sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/lewontins-paradox-of-variation--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Simulation bound on how much linked selection can flatten π across N" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/lewontins-paradox-of-variation> ;
    dct:description "Settle whether linked selection can close the gap by computing the most it could do under generous but empirically bounded parameters. Use SLiM forward simulation with tree-sequence recording plus msprime recapitation, and stdpopsim genome models and DFEs where available, across a ladder of census sizes N from 10^3 to 10^7 simulated directly. Extrapolate to 10^12 with the analytic background-selection and sweep formulas (B = exp(−Σ u/(s(1+r/s)²)) plus a recurrent-sweep coalescent rate), calibrated against the simulations in the overlap range. Upper-bound parameters: the deleterious mutation rate at the top of published estimates, a DFE fraction of strongly selected new mutations at the top of estimates, and adaptive substitution rate α from McDonald–Kreitman ranges. Output: the curve π(N) and the maximum achievable flattening (slope of log π on log N over 10^4–10^12). Compare with the observed slope from approach 1 or from Buffalo 2021." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "The minimum π–N slope achievable by linked selection under upper-bound parameters. If that minimum exceeds the observed slope plus its CI, linked selection cannot explain the paradox, as Buffalo argued, and demography or μ must; if it falls below, the bound shows which parameter regime is needed." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Extrapolation from 10^7 to 10^12 is unreliable: the analytic model misfits simulations at the top of the ladder, or sweep interference (genetic draft saturation) makes predictions model-dependent across plausible formulas." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/simulation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Choose 3 representative genome architectures (vertebrate, insect, plant) from stdpopsim; fix parameter ranges from literature before simulating. 2. Run SLiM simulations over the N ladder with 20 replicates each; compute π at neutral sites. 3. Calibrate the analytic B and sweep model to simulations; extrapolate to large N. 4. Compute the maximum flattening and compare with the observed slope." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): hard" ;
    skos:note "Missing: Direct simulation at N ≥ 10^8 is infeasible, so the bound rests on extrapolation." ;
    skos:note "Tool: simulation" ;
    skos:note "Tool: population-genetics" ;
    skos:note "Tool: large-scale-computation" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/kimura-heterozygosity> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/kimura-neutral-theory> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/kimura-substitution-rate> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/MesserLab/SLiM> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://tskit.dev/msprime/docs/stable/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://popsim-consortium.github.io/stdpopsim-docs/stable/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.76065> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msy092> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/lewontins-paradox-of-variation> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Lewontin's paradox of variation" .


  <https://github.com/MesserLab/SLiM> dct:bibliographicCitation "SLiM forward population-genetic simulator" .
  <https://tskit.dev/msprime/docs/stable/> dct:bibliographicCitation "msprime coalescent simulator for recapitation" .
  <https://popsim-consortium.github.io/stdpopsim-docs/stable/> dct:bibliographicCitation "stdpopsim species models and DFEs" .
  <https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.76065> dct:title "Broad-scale variation in human genetic diversity levels is predicted by purifying selection on coding and non-coding elements" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Murphy, Elyashiv, Amster and Sella 2023, purifying selection predicts human diversity landscapes (calibration of B maps)" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msy092> dct:title "The Neutral Theory in Light of Natural Selection" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Kern and Hahn 2018, The neutral theory in light of natural selection" .
}

